{
  "abstract": "Can a finite operation on a regular-chain sextuple preserve all fifteen individual rational square roots while changing the entries? We give an explicit affirmative construction. A surface with two square equations decodes to six rational entries satisfying three regular-quadruple relations and one regular-quintuple relation. We supply every pair root and all denominator guards. The parameter involution $(r,t)\\mapsto(1/(2r),-4(t+1)/(3t+4))$ lifts by multiplying each of the two source roots by $t/[2(t+1)]$. Exact polynomial identities prove that it squares to the identity and transports the complete root data. Distinctness is a separate condition: some old controls map to collisions, while two displayed source points have nondegenerate images, including an all-positive sextuple. One application to an archived 1124-row corpus produces fourteen distinct admissible image classes, ten absent from that corpus; no literature-wide novelty of individual examples is claimed. We also prove three explicit rational isomorphisms between triple-induced elliptic curves of the source and image, connecting two of them to the established distinguished-point-preserving isomorphic-triple method. A descent-coset adjustment transports three individual extension roots, and matching two such transports supplies a conditional five-root interface for a seventh entry. All universal identities and finite controls have compact independent exact-arithmetic certificates. We construct neither a rational septuple nor an infinite family by iteration, and the tested seventh-entry operations produce no new twenty-root point.",
